{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Isaac entreated HaShem opposite his wife, for she was barren.</b> The verse should properly have preceded and said, 'And Rebekah was barren,' and afterward, 'And Isaac entreated HaShem, for she was barren.' The reason he placed the prayer first may be explained as his placing the principal matter first. It teaches us that the intention is not that the barrenness was the cause of the prayer — for if so, the cause would be principal and the prayer secondary to it — but the intention of the verse's language is that the prayer was the cause of the barrenness, to tell you that she was made barren only so that they would both pray about the matter. Therefore he placed the prayer first, for it is the principal and the primary cause for which the barrenness came upon our mother Rebekah. This is what the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'Why were the matriarchs barren? Because the Holy One, blessed be He, desires the prayers of the righteous.' From here one may further learn the power of prayer, which is very great and can even change nature. For this reason he used the expression ויעתר and did not say ויתפלל or ויצעק or other expressions, for it is from the root עתר (a shovel/ox-goad used to turn grain). Thus the Sages, of blessed memory, understood it, as they said in the first chapter of Sukkah (14a): 'Why is the prayer of the righteous compared to an עתר? Just as this עתר turns the grain from place to place, so the prayer of the righteous turns the mind of the Holy One, blessed be He, from the attribute of cruelty to the attribute of mercy.' Its meaning is: just as the עתר turns the grain from below to above and then makes it fall from above to below, so the righteous person in his prayer — his thought wanders and rises from below to above, and afterward he draws down and brings the influence from above to below. And they also explained it from the language of abundance of prayer, from the expression (Ezekiel 8:11) ״ועתר ענן הקטרת עולה״, and further from the language of a breach/tunnel, in the manner they interpreted concerning Manasseh." "<b>And know</b> that of the four matriarchs, three of them were barren: Sarah, as it is written (Genesis 11:30) ״ותהי שרי עקרה״; Rebekah, of whom it is written ״כי עקרה היא״; Rachel, as it is said (Genesis 29) ״ורחל עקרה״." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat), one may give a reason for the barrenness of the matriarchs. Sarah was barren for two reasons: the first, so that Ishmael would come forth from Abraham, as it is said (Genesis 16:2) ״הנה נא עצרני ה׳ מלדת בא נא אל שפחתי״; the second, in order to change her name. And why was the change of her name necessary? To demonstrate true faith, for since the world was created with the letter ה, thus the power of the mazal (celestial destiny) was nullified through the letter ה. It teaches us that the Master who ordained the arrangement of the stars is the one who overcomes and nullifies it at His will, and there is none besides Him. And Rebekah too — it is known that Isaac was forty years old when he took her, and when he begot Jacob and Esau he was sixty years old; accordingly, she spent twenty years with him without giving birth. And the reason for her barrenness was so that Esau would not go forth into evil conduct during Abraham's lifetime, for the Holy One, blessed be He, had already promised him, as He said to him (Genesis 15:15) ״ואתה תבא אל אבותיך בשלום״, and Ishmael repented in his days. And on the day that Abraham departed, on that very day Esau went forth into evil conduct — and that is the Lentil Stew that Jacob made. And the reason in Rachel's case was so that she would be the cause for Bilhah and Zilpah, from whom came forth Dan, Naphtali, Gad, and Asher."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "Sukkah 14a citation matches the Talmudic passage referenced.", 2 "Comment closes without trailing colon or continuation marker; paragraph ends cleanly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:10.814937280Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}